		"text": "Exploring Capgemini’s A World in Balance 2026 Report\n\nWater stress, severe climate disruption, resource constraints and geopolitical volatility have made business resilience transformation a necessity.\n\nCorporate sustainability has evolved far beyond a compliance checklist, emerging as a core discipline focused on crafting climate resilience strategies to maintain growth and business performance.\n\nCapgemini’s fifth annual report, A World in Balance 2026, explores this vital transition.\n\nDrawing on a survey of 2,100 senior executives from 701 organisations across 13 countries, in-depth interviews with corporate leaders and insights from 6,500 global consumers, the report provides a comprehensive blueprint for navigating an uncertain economic landscape.\n\nEscalating physical risks and climate threat\n\nClimate-related disruptions are increasingly impacting everyday operations, supply chains and long-term growth prospects.\n\nNearly nine in ten organisations (87%) report climate-related supply chain disruptions, highlighting widespread vulnerability across global trade networks.\n\nSimultaneously, water stress has risen to the top of the strategic agenda. 61% of executives believe that water scarcity will become a greater constraint on business growth than energy availability within five years, driving the rapid adoption of water risk management plans.\n\nIn response to these pressures, 64% of organisations report revising their sustainability priorities , while 78% are actively integrating energy and resource resilience directly into their corporate strategies.\n\nCyril Garcia, Head of Global Sustainability Services and Corporate Responsibility at Capgemini, says: “Sustainability is entering a new era. Organisations must move beyond compliance and reporting to build resilience, secure critical resources, create long-term business value and ultimately preserve their sovereignty.\n\n“AI can accelerate this transition, but lasting impact will depend on how effectively organisations embed sustainability into their operations, decisions and innovation strategies.”\n\nFar from being an administrative cost centre, sustainability is increasingly recognised as a key driver of business value creation.\n\nAlmost seven in ten (69%) organisations report a positive return on investment from their sustainability initiatives, representing a marked increase from 49% in 2025.\n\nThe operational and commercial benefits are widespread: 67% of companies report lower operating costs – achieving average savings of 6% over the past year – while 64% state that sustainability investments have contributed to increased sales.\n\nBy linking environmental actions to financial outcomes, businesses are unlocking new opportunities for efficiency, customer acquisition and brand equity.\n\nDespite sustained investment momentum, turning sustainability ambitions into operational reality remains challenging.\n\nCapgemini’s sustainability maturity index declined for a second consecutive year, falling from 111 in 2025 to 105 in 2026.\n\nThis widening gap between ambition and execution is particularly acute within net zero programmes.\n\nThe proportion of organisations falling behind on their net zero targets rose from 1% in 2025 to 11% in 2026, while 29% have postponed their net zero objectives compared to just 8% a year ago.\n\nMany businesses continue to struggle with fragmented data, limited visibility across value chains and difficulties in managing Scope 3 emissions.\n\nAiman Ezzat, CEO of Capgemini, said on LinkedIn: “Some say sustainability has slipped down the corporate agenda. Our latest research tells a different story.\n\n“Wider global trends explain why leaders are investing. Sustainability is increasingly viewed as a resilience strategy, not just an environmental commitment. After this year’s exceptional summer, it’s perhaps easier to understand why 71% of executives now say securing access to critical resources has a greater influence on their sustainability decisions than emissions targets alone.\n\n“In a world defined by volatility, sustainability and business resilience are converging into a single strategic discipline. Leaders who treat them as separate agendas risk missing where real competitive advantage now lies.”\n\nAI is emerging as a powerful execution engine for sustainability, helping organisations optimise supply chain resilience, improve energy efficiency , refine reporting and forecast climate risks.\n\nMore than 70% of executives believe the benefits of generative AI outweigh its environmental impact.\n\nNevertheless, rapid AI deployment brings new environmental and governance considerations, particularly regarding resource consumption.\n\nMost organisations still lack visibility into AI's footprint: only 38% measure the energy consumption of AI systems, 34% track AI-related carbon emissions and just 25% disclose AI-related water usage.\n\nTo navigate these complexities, the report outlines five priority actions for leaders across strategy, operations, supply chain, procurement, risk management, product development, finance and technology:\n\nUltimately, building a resilient enterprise requires moving beyond passive reporting to embed climate adaptation into every operational decision.\n\nExecutive Board Member, Head of Sustainability and Corporate Responsibility"
